{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 7, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era marked by rising Islamic power under Saladin. His life story involves complex political maneuvering and military leadership that shaped the fate of the Crusader states in the Levant. Raymond inherited his title after his father was killed by the Order of Assassins and he immediately faced challenges regarding the stability of his county along with broader regional conflicts.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond spent part of his youth as a hostage in Muslim captivity following the capture of his father yet he eventually secured his freedom to assume control over Tripoli. He married Hodierna of Jerusalem who was the daughter of King Baldwin II and this union strengthened his ties to the royal family while solidifying his claim to power. His early reign focused on consolidating authority within his own lands and building alliances with other noble families across the kingdom to ensure mutual defense against external threats.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe count acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule independently during certain periods of his life. Raymond found himself at odds with other factions at court including the Templars and supporters of Guy of Lusignan who sought greater influence over royal decisions. These internal divisions weakened the kingdom significantly because noble houses prioritized personal gain over unified strategy against the growing Ayyubid forces led by Saladin. Raymond often advocated for caution and diplomacy rather than rash military action but his advice was frequently ignored by more aggressive elements within the nobility.\n\n*Battle of Hattin and Legacy*\nRaymond commanded a major contingent of troops during the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader army suffered a catastrophic defeat at the hands of Saladin. He managed to escape the encirclement while many others were captured or killed including King Guy and true cross relics. This loss paved the way for Saladin to conquer Jerusalem and most other Crusader holdings shortly thereafter. Raymond died later that same year possibly from illness or despair over the collapse of the kingdom he tried to protect. His legacy remains that of a pragmatic leader who understood the limitations of Crusader power yet could not prevent the ultimate downfall due to factional strife and superior enemy coordination. Historians view him as one of the most capable Frankish leaders of his time despite the tragic outcome of his efforts to preserve the Latin East.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -2.8372213e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.00010860556}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.019528167}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.25194278}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -7.7727476e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.7881409e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.7881409e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -3.9339143e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0010259644}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.0248919}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.008953179